<section class="home-introduction" id="understanding" aria-labelledby="home-intro-title">
  <h2 id="home-intro-title">What is<br><em>Sanatan Dharma?</em></h2>
  <div><p class="home-standfirst">A diverse family of spiritual traditions, united by enduring questions about life, the self and the Divine.</p><p>Sanatana Dharma is a name used for Hinduism, often translated as “eternal dharma.” Its traditions encompass philosophy, devotion, ritual and ways of living. They share a rich inheritance while offering different interpretations of spiritual truth.</p><p>Begin with four ideas that appear throughout its teachings. Their meanings deepen across different texts and schools.</p><a class="home-inline-link" href="/understanding.html">Read the full introduction <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
  <dl class="home-ideas" aria-label="Four foundational ideas">
    <div><dt>Dharma</dt><dd>Ethical living, responsibility and the principles that sustain life.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Karma</dt><dd>Action and its consequences, shaping the journey of life.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Samsara</dt><dd>The cycle of birth, death and rebirth in Hindu thought.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Moksha</dt><dd>Liberation from that cycle, understood differently across schools.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</section>
<section class="home-divine" id="divine" aria-labelledby="home-divine-title">
  <div class="home-divine-intro"><h2 id="home-divine-title">Many forms.<br><em>Many ways of devotion.</em></h2><p>Divine traditions are an entrance into a much larger world of stories and thought. These are a few starting points, rather than a complete list.</p><a class="home-inline-link" href="/deities.html">Explore gods and goddesses <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
  <div class="home-divine-names"><a href="/deities.html#shiva"><span lang="sa">शिव</span><div><h3>Shiva</h3><p>Explore Shaiva traditions</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="/deities.html#vishnu"><span lang="sa">विष्णु</span><div><h3>Vishnu</h3><p>Explore Vaishnava traditions</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="/deities.html#devi"><span lang="sa">देवी</span><div><h3>Devi</h3><p>Explore Shakta traditions</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
  <details class="home-divine-question"><summary>How many gods and goddesses are there?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div><p>There is no single count that captures every Hindu tradition. Texts and communities describe the Divine in different ways, and many traditions connect multiple divine forms with one ultimate reality.</p><p>In the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (3.9.1–9), a dialogue moves through several numbers—including thirty-three—and ultimately speaks of one. It invites reflection on the relationship between multiplicity and unity, rather than providing a universal catalogue.</p></div></details>
</section>
